{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0e01e61c7de584ed0169bb16315478819195d9cd17627b1031", "created": 1773868593, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**BIOPSY** is a medical diagnostic procedure in which a clinician removes a sample of tissue, cells, or sometimes fluid from a suspected tumor or other abnormal area (such as breast tissue, cervix, skin, prostate, lymph nodes, bone marrow, bladder, thyroid nodules, or metastatic sites) so it can be examined in a laboratory—typically by a pathologist under a microscope (cytology/histology)—to determine whether cancer is present, what type it is, and what features it has. Across many cancers, BIOPSY is described as the definitive or most reliable way to confirm a diagnosis (for example, repeatedly emphasized as the main/only way to accurately confirm primary CNS lymphoma and prostate cancer), and its results commonly guide subsequent treatment planning.\n\nBIOPSY may be performed in multiple ways depending on the organ and clinical situation, including needle techniques (such as fine-needle aspiration), excisional sampling of lymph nodes, skin or cervical biopsies, bone marrow biopsy, and biopsies performed during surgery or endoscopic procedures (e.g., during cystoscopy, proctoscopy, direct laryngoscopy/endoscopy). For certain settings (e.g., thyroid nodules), the decision to biopsy may be based on imaging features and size criteria. Imaging (mammogram, ultrasound, MRI, CT, EUS, etc.) can be used before the procedure to determine necessity, during the procedure to guide accurate sampling, and afterward to help evaluate disease extent for planning; clips/markers may be placed at the sampled site to aid future imaging or treatment.\n\nBIOPSY findings are repeatedly described as key inputs for staging (including TNM staging and clinical staging in head and neck cancers) and for determining tumor grade and other risk features, especially after surgical removal when the postoperative specimen is analyzed. The sampled tissue can also be used for biomarker and molecular/genomic testing (e.g., receptor status such as ER/PR/HER2, protein/biomarker profiling, and other tests that influence targeted therapy choices), making BIOPSY important not only for diagnosis but also for selecting therapies and confirming suspected progression when follow-up suggests worsening disease.\n\nTiming and clinical context vary: in ovarian cancer, BIOPSY is often done during initial surgery, but may be required before starting chemotherapy if surgery is not the first step or if disease is extensive; in prostate cancer, it may be recommended after PSA testing/DRE/imaging and can be repeated as a confirmatory biopsy over time. In primary CNS lymphoma, BIOPSY is highlighted as crucial for confirmation and may involve brain tissue or, in some contexts, vitreous biopsy; steroid therapy is noted as something that should not begin until after BIOPSY and diagnosis in that context. BIOPSY is invasive and may carry risks such as pain, bleeding, and infection; for some cancers (e.g., bone cancer), careful planning and experienced performance at a specialized center are emphasized.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 596, "prompt_tokens": 2448, "total_tokens": 3044, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}